{
  "summary": "This computer looks in good shape: there are only three findings, all low or informational, and each looks like normal activity. The most worthwhile thing to do is confirm you recognize the extra administrator account <computer>\\<account-2>. The other two items are changes since last week's scan that match ordinary software updates.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Extra administrator account to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The account <computer>\\<account-2> has full administrator rights, which means it can change anything on the computer. It is a local account and was not newly added since the last scan (new admins would be reported at high). Having a separate admin account is actually good practice. Just make sure this is an account you or someone in your household set up. If you don't recognize it, treat that seriously and follow the fix shown in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Windows Defender service now points to a newer version",
      "finding_ids": [
        "CHANGED-PERSISTENCE:47c86358"
      ],
      "explanation": "The Windows Defender (WinDefend) service now starts the program from a folder with a newer version number (4.18.25010-1 changed to 4.18.25020-2). The location is the same and only the version folder changed, which is exactly what a normal Defender platform update looks like. The rule flags any changed auto-start entry because swapping the program behind a trusted name is a known trick, but nothing here suggests that. No action is likely needed."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "New Google updater scheduled task",
      "finding_ids": [
        "NEW-PERSISTENCE:c74ac833"
      ],
      "explanation": "A scheduled task called GoogleUpdaterTaskSystem appeared since the last scan. It runs Google's updater from the standard Program Files (x86) location. This is typically added when Google software such as Chrome is installed or updated. If you use Google software, this is expected and harmless. If you don't, check the report's details for this item."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
